## Chapter 3 — Literature Survey

### 3.1 Existing Systems

| System | Description | Limitations |
|---|---|---|
| **One Tree Planted** | Donation-based tree planting NGO | No individual tracking; no adoption model |
| **iNaturalist** | Citizen-science species identification | No adoption workflow; no NGO integration |
| **Treemapper (Plant-for-the-Planet)** | Tree planting verification app | Limited social features; no community feed |
| **Adopt-a-Tree.ca** | Municipal tree adoption portals | Geographically limited; no tech stack |
| **Google Earth Engine** | Satellite-based vegetation monitoring | No individual-level tracking; enterprise tool |

### 3.2 Identified Gaps

1. **No unified platform** combines NGO management, citizen adoption, and community engagement.
2. **Geospatial discovery** is absent from most plant adoption workflows.
3. **Real-time growth tracking** with structured health reports is not offered.
4. **AI-based plant identification** integrated within adoption platforms is a novel contribution.
5. **Social features** (feed, likes, follows, bookmarks) are not present in existing environmental platforms.

### 3.3 Technologies Reviewed

| Technology | Purpose | Selection Rationale |
|---|---|---|
| Next.js 15 (App Router) | Frontend framework | Server-side rendering, React 19, file-based routing |
| Express.js 4.x | Backend framework | Mature, lightweight, middleware-driven architecture |
| Supabase | Backend-as-a-Service | PostgreSQL, Auth, Storage, RLS — all-in-one |
| PostGIS | Geospatial extension | Industry-standard spatial indexing and queries |
| Leaflet.js | Map rendering | Open-source, lightweight, well-documented |
| Tailwind CSS 4 | Utility-first CSS | Rapid prototyping, consistent design tokens |
| n8n | AI workflow orchestration | No-code webhook pipelines for PlantNet + Gemini |

